{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>But the midwives feared Elohim.</b> As it is written (Ecclesiastes 8:2): ״אֲנִי פִּי מֶלֶךְ שְׁמוֹר וְעַל דִּבְרַת שְׁבוּעַת אֱלֹהִים״ — 'Guard the mouth of the king, and concerning the matter of the oath of Elohim.' For in this manner you guard the decree of the king — when it does not contravene the oath of Elohim that the One who adjured us adjured us to keep all His commandments. Thus the midwives feared Elohim and placed their lives in danger, for murder is one of those three for which one must be killed rather than transgress, for 'what do you see that your blood is redder?' and so forth (Pesachim 25b). And according to the opinion of our Sages of blessed memory, who said (Sotah 11b) that Shifrah is so called because she beautifies (מְשַׁפֶּרֶת) the infant, and Puah from the language of speech (לְשׁוֹן דִּבּוּר) toward the child — it appears to me in this regard that what they did was rebuff Pharaoh with a difficulty and say, 'כִּי חָיוֹת הֵנָּה בְּטֶרֶם תָּבוֹא אֲלֵיהֶן הַמְיַלֶּדֶת' and so forth. And the question is difficult: if so, what advantage did they derive from being midwives, given that the Hebrew women do not need them? To this Scripture comes as a defender, saying that they do not deliver the Hebrew women, for חָיוֹת הֵנָּה — they are like beasts; rather, their principal task is to beautify the infant and to cry out to it after it has already been born. And Pharaoh had said, 'בְּיַלֶּדְכֶן אֶת הָעִבְרִיּוֹת' and so forth, and from this they found an opening for excuse, to say: we do not deliver them. Therefore they fell silent and answered Pharaoh nothing immediately, so that he would not appoint other midwives — and he deceived himself. Therefore ״וַיֵּיטֶב אֱלֹהִים לַמְיַלְּדוֹת וַיִּרֶב הָעָם וַיַּעַצְמוּ מְאֹד״ — 'Elohim dealt well with the midwives, and the people multiplied and grew very mighty' — for this is good for them, because בְּרוֹב עָם הַדְרַת מֶלֶךְ — 'in the multitude of people is the splendor of the king' — and from them emerged kings and priests, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וַיַּעַשׂ לָהֶם בָּתִּים״. And likewise it is good for the priests and for the Levites, for with a multitude of people they give priestly gifts and Levitical gifts."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:50.616493469Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}